WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.675
&gt;&gt; My name is Amar and I lead

00:00:24.676 --> 00:00:27.795
Project Fiddle here at
Microsoft research.

00:00:27.796 --> 00:00:31.620
My colleagues and
I are working on

00:00:31.621 --> 00:00:36.660
speeding up distributed
Machine Learning jobs.

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:41.560
Our emphasis has been on
Distributed Deep Learning.

00:00:41.561 --> 00:00:45.645
DNN models are
expensive to train,

00:00:45.646 --> 00:00:49.215
not just in the amount of
resources they consume,

00:00:49.216 --> 00:00:50.750
but also in the amount
of time they

00:00:50.751 --> 00:00:52.935
take to train these DNN models.

00:00:52.936 --> 00:00:55.590
So, the motivation
for these systems,

00:00:55.591 --> 00:00:57.230
we have this new generation of

00:00:57.231 --> 00:01:00.860
Big Data systems in
this generation of AI.

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:05.875
We have tasks such as image
recognition classification,

00:01:05.876 --> 00:01:08.630
translation, and speech-to-text

00:01:08.631 --> 00:01:10.325
and text-to-speech conversion.

00:01:10.326 --> 00:01:12.140
There have been various solutions

00:01:12.141 --> 00:01:13.610
that have been proposed
over the years.

00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:15.500
So, I think the first
generation of solutions

00:01:15.501 --> 00:01:17.690
like disbelief and Project Adam

00:01:17.691 --> 00:01:20.720
looked at using commodity
computers to solve some of

00:01:20.721 --> 00:01:24.975
these problems using DNNs.

00:01:24.976 --> 00:01:27.020
In the next generation of
systems like TensorFlow

00:01:27.021 --> 00:01:29.415
and Microsoft CNTK were

00:01:29.416 --> 00:01:33.170
generalizations that
let the programmers

00:01:33.171 --> 00:01:35.270
specify how to code

00:01:35.271 --> 00:01:38.630
up these DNNs and solve many

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:40.110
of these stocks as opposed to

00:01:40.111 --> 00:01:42.525
buying solutions
for specific tasks.

00:01:42.526 --> 00:01:46.490
But the key factor
that still remains is

00:01:46.491 --> 00:01:50.250
DNN training is extremely time
and resource consuming.

00:01:50.251 --> 00:01:57.245
So, training jobs last days
to multiple weeks at times,

00:01:57.246 --> 00:02:00.820
and there's this constant cycle
of train validate

00:02:00.821 --> 00:02:05.780
repeat which blows up the
training time effectively.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.940
One of the most important
things in this work

00:02:10.941 --> 00:02:13.070
is naively
parallelizing this work

00:02:13.071 --> 00:02:14.510
can actually be detrimental,

00:02:14.511 --> 00:02:17.180
because what really matters
at the end of the day is

00:02:17.181 --> 00:02:20.510
the quality of work or time
to reach a certain accuracy.

00:02:20.511 --> 00:02:23.000
So, the problem that we're
trying to solve here

00:02:23.001 --> 00:02:25.130
is we've set an ambitious
goal for ourselves,

00:02:25.131 --> 00:02:28.045
we'd like to perform
100X more efficient training.

00:02:28.046 --> 00:02:30.500
Intuitively, what
this means is if you

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:33.130
have a model that
takes an entire day to

00:02:33.131 --> 00:02:34.550
train we'd like to train

00:02:34.551 --> 00:02:38.150
this model in 15 minutes
over your coffee break.

00:02:38.151 --> 00:02:39.920
The approach we take in

00:02:39.921 --> 00:02:43.360
Project Fiddle is to cut
across the system stack.

00:02:43.361 --> 00:02:46.790
So, we look at training,

00:02:46.791 --> 00:02:49.940
the bigger picture
of training from

00:02:49.941 --> 00:02:53.940
a single GPU all the way
up to multi GPU,

00:02:53.941 --> 00:02:57.040
multi-machine clusters
across the system stack.

00:02:57.041 --> 00:02:58.825
So, we look at
the memory subsystem,

00:02:58.826 --> 00:03:00.830
we look at how to structure

00:03:00.831 --> 00:03:03.680
computation across these
different computational units,

00:03:03.681 --> 00:03:06.650
we look at the communication
infrastructure

00:03:06.651 --> 00:03:07.820
so it's speeding up

00:03:07.821 --> 00:03:12.010
communication which is
inherent in this workload.

00:03:12.011 --> 00:03:14.275
Then finally, all
the way up the stack,

00:03:14.276 --> 00:03:18.330
I've been talking about
these innovations

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.015
that help a single job,

00:03:20.016 --> 00:03:24.200
but we look at sort of
multiple jobs all using

00:03:24.201 --> 00:03:25.580
the same cluster running in

00:03:25.581 --> 00:03:28.770
this multi-tenant
clustered environment,

00:03:28.771 --> 00:03:31.565
and we were looking
at techniques to make

00:03:31.566 --> 00:03:35.450
fault-tolerant elastic training
work on these clusters.

00:03:35.451 --> 00:03:37.999
Let me take a very
specific example

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.500
to keep you grounded
in an example.

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.680
So, one of the projects, Gist,

00:03:46.681 --> 00:03:50.375
looks at how much more can
we do on a single GPU?

00:03:50.376 --> 00:03:52.280
So, before you ask for

00:03:52.281 --> 00:03:54.650
your second GPU we'd
like to make sure

00:03:54.651 --> 00:03:59.000
that you're maxing out the
capabilities of a single GPU.

00:03:59.001 --> 00:04:02.675
So, let's move here
while I explain Gist.

00:04:02.676 --> 00:04:04.430
The first thing to note here

00:04:04.431 --> 00:04:08.745
is what I'm showing
you here is memory,

00:04:08.746 --> 00:04:11.655
is a bottleneck for
even single GPU training.

00:04:11.656 --> 00:04:15.750
So, sorted on
this X-axis we've sorted

00:04:15.751 --> 00:04:18.605
different DNNs for
image classification

00:04:18.606 --> 00:04:21.140
effectively sorted
them over time.

00:04:21.141 --> 00:04:24.620
You'll note that
the memory consumed by

00:04:24.621 --> 00:04:29.140
these different DNNs
over time increases.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.640
We further dug deeper

00:04:31.641 --> 00:04:34.100
and we wanted to find
out what in there is

00:04:34.101 --> 00:04:38.080
consuming memory and the stuff
that's marked in red,

00:04:38.081 --> 00:04:39.845
that's highlighted in red,

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:41.665
are the heavy hitters.

00:04:41.666 --> 00:04:46.485
Those are the feature maps
that are stored

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:52.180
and needed for backward
computation during DNN training.

00:04:52.181 --> 00:04:57.560
So, to explain this in
sort of an abstract way,

00:04:57.561 --> 00:05:00.260
here's the representation
of a DNN layer,

00:05:00.261 --> 00:05:02.060
you can think of
any layer taking as

00:05:02.061 --> 00:05:05.015
input X outputting
some feature map Y.

00:05:05.016 --> 00:05:06.860
Then in its backward pass

00:05:06.861 --> 00:05:08.300
it's going to take
some output gradient

00:05:08.301 --> 00:05:12.535
dy and output sort
of this gradient dx,

00:05:12.536 --> 00:05:15.170
and dx is a function
of xy and dy,

00:05:15.171 --> 00:05:17.085
it could be a function
of xy and dy.

00:05:17.086 --> 00:05:18.880
So, to compute dx now,

00:05:18.881 --> 00:05:21.365
we need to actually
store X and Y.

00:05:21.366 --> 00:05:24.795
So, this is the abstract view
of what the layer does.

00:05:24.796 --> 00:05:26.940
If you unroll this over time,

00:05:26.941 --> 00:05:28.930
this is the same
representation of that,

00:05:28.931 --> 00:05:30.460
we've unrolled it over time,

00:05:30.461 --> 00:05:33.980
Li here takes as input X
and outputs Y and much

00:05:33.981 --> 00:05:35.945
later in its backward pass

00:05:35.946 --> 00:05:38.585
takes as input dy and outputs dx.

00:05:38.586 --> 00:05:40.790
So if you just looked at

00:05:40.791 --> 00:05:43.180
the lifetime of
this data structure X,

00:05:43.181 --> 00:05:45.780
it's first generated here,

00:05:45.781 --> 00:05:49.090
and then used here
and discarded here.

00:05:49.091 --> 00:05:50.790
But we know better,

00:05:50.791 --> 00:05:54.280
X is actually just used
in this time slot,

00:05:54.281 --> 00:05:58.215
not used in this long gap,

00:05:58.216 --> 00:06:01.800
and then used again here during
the backward pass of Li.

00:06:01.801 --> 00:06:03.410
So, now we have an opportunity

00:06:03.411 --> 00:06:06.095
here to take this data structure,

00:06:06.096 --> 00:06:08.110
encode it, and hopefully,

00:06:08.111 --> 00:06:11.300
have a succinct
representation of X.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:15.005
In this long time gap relinquish

00:06:15.006 --> 00:06:18.260
all the memory that X
was consuming and then

00:06:18.261 --> 00:06:21.680
decode that encoded
representation

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:23.600
into a full-fledged
representation

00:06:23.601 --> 00:06:26.175
again when we need to do
backward computation.

00:06:26.176 --> 00:06:29.190
So, just takes in

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.670
the original
computation graph for

00:06:31.671 --> 00:06:34.640
a particular task
and statically for

00:06:34.641 --> 00:06:36.980
every data structure augments

00:06:36.981 --> 00:06:38.990
this computational graph
to have these encode

00:06:38.991 --> 00:06:41.960
decode edges and
our memory manager

00:06:41.961 --> 00:06:44.860
effectively manages
all these memory regions.

00:06:44.861 --> 00:06:47.960
I was hand waving here when
I talked about encode and

00:06:47.961 --> 00:06:51.645
decode functions,
but specifically,

00:06:51.646 --> 00:06:53.550
we have a bunch of techniques,

00:06:53.551 --> 00:06:56.655
both lossless and
lossy techniques,

00:06:56.656 --> 00:07:00.280
that are used for these
encode and decode functions.

00:07:00.281 --> 00:07:03.180
The higher order bit
here is using

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:06.500
Gist for these networks

00:07:06.501 --> 00:07:08.965
we can get up to
a 2x compression ratio.

00:07:08.966 --> 00:07:10.510
Encode decode of course is not

00:07:10.511 --> 00:07:14.405
free for these
different networks,

00:07:14.406 --> 00:07:16.340
there's a minimal overhead of

00:07:16.341 --> 00:07:17.950
one to seven percent that we pay

00:07:17.951 --> 00:07:19.610
for the same mini batch size.

00:07:19.611 --> 00:07:21.935
But the beauty of Gist is we can

00:07:21.936 --> 00:07:25.660
enable larger mini batch
sizes to be loaded,

00:07:25.661 --> 00:07:28.010
and so with larger
mini batch sizes

00:07:28.011 --> 00:07:30.700
will have faster training.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.420
So, this was Gist which
is one extreme of

00:07:34.421 --> 00:07:39.350
the spectrum making a single
GPU do more effectively.

00:07:39.351 --> 00:07:41.539
Let me take you to
this other extreme

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.520
which is Multi-Machine Training.

00:07:44.521 --> 00:07:49.245
So, parallel programming
is hard in general,

00:07:49.246 --> 00:07:52.520
and I want to showcase why it's

00:07:52.521 --> 00:07:56.810
harder parallelizing
DNN training.

00:07:56.811 --> 00:07:59.780
So, for that let's move on
to this particular terminal.

00:07:59.781 --> 00:08:01.550
I'm gonna skip ahead
a couple of slides

00:08:01.551 --> 00:08:04.190
to get those slide where I
talk about parallel training.

00:08:04.191 --> 00:08:06.415
So, let me talk about PipeDream

00:08:06.416 --> 00:08:10.605
which is a new way to
parallelize DNN computation.

00:08:10.606 --> 00:08:13.970
So, the most important thing to

00:08:13.971 --> 00:08:17.615
note here is naive parallelization
can hurt DNN training.

00:08:17.616 --> 00:08:19.030
Because if you naively

00:08:19.031 --> 00:08:20.900
parallelize something
you might actually be

00:08:20.901 --> 00:08:22.745
increasing the total time

00:08:22.746 --> 00:08:25.100
for training because
total time of training depends

00:08:25.101 --> 00:08:26.965
on two things time per

00:08:26.966 --> 00:08:29.990
Epoch and the number of
Epochs for a given accuracy.

00:08:29.991 --> 00:08:32.150
While you might
decrease the time per

00:08:32.151 --> 00:08:33.230
Epoch you might be

00:08:33.231 --> 00:08:35.480
increasing the number
of Epochs effective.

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:38.020
One of the predominant ways
of parallelizing

00:08:38.021 --> 00:08:40.010
DNN is this thing called
data parallel training,

00:08:40.011 --> 00:08:41.975
where you run multiple replicas

00:08:41.976 --> 00:08:43.610
and these replicas communicated

00:08:43.611 --> 00:08:45.115
with each other every so often.

00:08:45.116 --> 00:08:47.270
If you aggregate
all the times for

00:08:47.271 --> 00:08:49.520
compute and communication styles,

00:08:49.521 --> 00:08:51.500
you notice this pattern
where there is

00:08:51.501 --> 00:08:54.230
a bunch of time you're
spending just communicating.

00:08:54.231 --> 00:08:57.830
So the problem there
of course is that of

00:08:57.831 --> 00:08:59.990
hardware efficiency or
waiting for communication to

00:08:59.991 --> 00:09:02.545
occur and you're not
doing any compute.

00:09:02.546 --> 00:09:05.720
This is including optimizations

00:09:05.721 --> 00:09:08.640
like wait freebie
back propagation,

00:09:08.641 --> 00:09:12.360
but try to pipeline
communication and computation.

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:17.455
So, this was a fake graph let
me show you a real graph.

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:20.165
Here's different networks

00:09:20.166 --> 00:09:22.700
image classification
RNNs that we tried out,

00:09:22.701 --> 00:09:24.200
and one thing that
you'll notice is

00:09:24.201 --> 00:09:25.880
as you increase
the number of workers,

00:09:25.881 --> 00:09:28.095
let's just consider
one of those DNNs,

00:09:28.096 --> 00:09:29.360
as you increase the number of

00:09:29.361 --> 00:09:32.645
workers for a given GPU setting,

00:09:32.646 --> 00:09:34.485
let's take K80s, for example,

00:09:34.486 --> 00:09:37.130
you'll notice that
the amount of time that's

00:09:37.131 --> 00:09:41.060
taken to communicate
these parameters

00:09:41.061 --> 00:09:43.260
for parameter exchange,
is going up.

00:09:43.261 --> 00:09:44.405
So, we're plotting communication

00:09:44.406 --> 00:09:46.975
overhead as a percentage
of total time,

00:09:46.976 --> 00:09:51.390
but what's worse is as you
start using faster compute.

00:09:51.391 --> 00:09:53.715
So if you go from K80 to B100s,

00:09:53.716 --> 00:09:56.000
your computer's going much
faster but the amount you're

00:09:56.001 --> 00:09:58.250
communicating is still the same,

00:09:58.251 --> 00:10:01.875
and so the communication
overhead shoots up dramatically.

00:10:01.876 --> 00:10:04.410
The other approach
that's used for

00:10:04.411 --> 00:10:06.675
parallel training is
a thing model parallelism,

00:10:06.676 --> 00:10:10.130
where you take a model

00:10:10.131 --> 00:10:13.680
and split it up across
sort of different GPUs.

00:10:13.681 --> 00:10:17.480
This is most commonly used to
work on memory limitations

00:10:17.481 --> 00:10:21.040
if you can't load
an entire model onto one GPU.

00:10:21.041 --> 00:10:22.850
So, this is great and enables you

00:10:22.851 --> 00:10:24.270
to at least train this model.

00:10:24.271 --> 00:10:28.480
But there are
two major challenges one,

00:10:28.481 --> 00:10:29.690
it's extremely hard for

00:10:29.691 --> 00:10:32.105
the programmer to figure out
where to split this model,

00:10:32.106 --> 00:10:34.010
this splits will vary not just

00:10:34.011 --> 00:10:36.185
based on the DNN
that's being trained,

00:10:36.186 --> 00:10:39.960
but also will vary based on
the deployment in question.

00:10:39.961 --> 00:10:43.270
The second is that of
low hardware efficiency.

00:10:43.271 --> 00:10:46.325
So, you'll notice
in this figure that

00:10:46.326 --> 00:10:48.170
only one GPU effectively is

00:10:48.171 --> 00:10:50.300
active at any given
point in time,

00:10:50.301 --> 00:10:51.780
and all the other GPUs
are sitting

00:10:51.781 --> 00:10:54.010
idle because of the nature
of this computation.

00:10:54.011 --> 00:10:58.070
To do backward work at machine
one for mini batch one it

00:10:58.071 --> 00:10:59.525
has to wait for

00:10:59.526 --> 00:11:01.010
the work to go through all the

00:11:01.011 --> 00:11:03.020
other machines and
come back to it.

00:11:03.021 --> 00:11:05.930
So, the idea we have in PipeDream

00:11:05.931 --> 00:11:10.370
is we're sort of

00:11:10.371 --> 00:11:13.395
going to take a re-look
at model parallelism.

00:11:13.396 --> 00:11:16.570
But not for memory limitations,

00:11:16.571 --> 00:11:18.600
but instead for throughput.

00:11:18.601 --> 00:11:20.260
We're going to use
this idea of pipeline,

00:11:20.261 --> 00:11:21.440
so we're going to add

00:11:21.441 --> 00:11:24.370
much more number of
jobs into the pipeline.

00:11:24.371 --> 00:11:26.575
At the same time,

00:11:26.576 --> 00:11:29.150
we'd like to have the programmer

00:11:29.151 --> 00:11:32.425
not have to specify how to
parallelize this workload.

00:11:32.426 --> 00:11:35.345
So, we have a tool that

00:11:35.346 --> 00:11:36.560
automatically figures out what

00:11:36.561 --> 00:11:38.720
the right parallelization
scheme is,

00:11:38.721 --> 00:11:40.490
and then a runtime
that executes this and

00:11:40.491 --> 00:11:44.120
the programmer is not involved
in this process at all.

00:11:44.121 --> 00:11:46.595
So, the way this is done is,

00:11:46.596 --> 00:11:47.750
I'm just going to skip to the

00:11:47.751 --> 00:11:49.290
slide that talks about this,

00:11:49.291 --> 00:11:50.975
we take a DNN Run,

00:11:50.976 --> 00:11:52.845
run it through our Profiler.

00:11:52.846 --> 00:11:55.925
This run is just on
one of the GPUs,

00:11:55.926 --> 00:11:56.960
it doesn't have to run on

00:11:56.961 --> 00:11:58.610
all the GPUs that you're
going to run this on.

00:11:58.611 --> 00:12:01.220
It spits out a profile of

00:12:01.221 --> 00:12:02.870
parallel times as well

00:12:02.871 --> 00:12:06.310
as how much data is
communicated between layers.

00:12:06.311 --> 00:12:08.420
Based on this information and

00:12:08.421 --> 00:12:09.410
given the number of machines

00:12:09.411 --> 00:12:10.909
that you want to run this on,

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:12.080
our optimizer can spit

00:12:12.081 --> 00:12:15.695
out a configuration
for parallelization.

00:12:15.696 --> 00:12:21.660
PipeDream combines model
parallelism, data parallelism,

00:12:21.661 --> 00:12:24.845
and pipelining, and
the speedups that we

00:12:24.846 --> 00:12:26.390
observe because of

00:12:26.391 --> 00:12:29.180
this combined pipelining
is significant.

00:12:29.181 --> 00:12:31.940
We get up to 5x speedup for

00:12:31.941 --> 00:12:35.120
various tasks that you've
tried this out for,

00:12:35.121 --> 00:12:40.005
be it image
classification or RNNs,

00:12:40.006 --> 00:12:42.650
for example,
sequence-to-sequence models

00:12:42.651 --> 00:12:46.455
that do video-to-text
transcription.

00:12:46.456 --> 00:12:49.300
So, to wrap things up,

00:12:49.301 --> 00:12:52.040
so in Project Fiddle

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:59.520
we're doing systems research
to speed up DNN training.

00:12:59.521 --> 00:13:03.755
We take a broader view
of training from

00:13:03.756 --> 00:13:08.685
single GPUs to multiple GPU,
multiple machine setup,

00:13:08.686 --> 00:13:11.120
and the cut across
the system stack from

00:13:11.121 --> 00:13:13.040
the memory subsystem to how

00:13:13.041 --> 00:13:15.295
the structure computation
automatically,

00:13:15.296 --> 00:13:18.470
to communication
that's inherent in

00:13:18.471 --> 00:13:21.815
this training paradigm
all the way

00:13:21.816 --> 00:13:25.730
up to fault-tolerant
elastic training

00:13:25.731 --> 00:13:29.230
on shared multi-tenant
clusters. Thanks.

